{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "177",
    "case_summary": "A 38-year-old woman was referred for evaluation of hypertension first noted at age 24 during pregnancy and again at age 38 after a basal ganglia hemorrhage. She had been diagnosed with splenomegaly of unknown cause at age 21; her younger brother had a similar finding. During her admission for hemorrhage, she underwent a decompressive craniectomy and was noted to have:  \n- Normochromic microcytic anemia and thrombocytopenia on full blood examination; other laboratory tests, including thyroid function, cortisol, aldosterone–renin ratio, urinary metanephrines, and renal function, were unremarkable.  \n- Lipid profile showed low HDL and elevated triglycerides.  \n- Renal Doppler ultrasound excluded renal artery stenosis but revealed incidental bilateral adrenal lesions and hepatosplenomegaly.  \n- Contrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen confirmed bilateral adrenal lesions (32 HU noncontrast; absolute washout 76.5%, relative washout 39%) that were unchanged after 8 months, and gross hepatosplenomegaly with dilated portal and splenic veins.  \nShe had no symptoms of adrenal hormone excess, and her blood pressure was controlled on a single antihypertensive agent.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Niemann-Pick disease type B\nB. Nonfunctioning adrenal adenomas considered because of incidental adrenal masses\nC. Gaucher disease considered in the context of hepatosplenomegaly and cytopenias\nD. Secondary causes of hypertension examined given her young age\nE. Niemann\nF. Genetic confirmation of Niemann\nG. None\nH. None\n",
